__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SAMPLES",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"COMING_SOON",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return n}});const t=[{q:"What should I revise first?"},{q:"Explain price elasticity of demand",a:"Price elasticity of demand (PED) measures how strongly quantity demanded responds to a change in price.\n\nPED = % change in quantity demanded \xf7 % change in price\n\n\u2022 It is negative for almost every good, because price and quantity move in opposite directions.\n\u2022 Bigger than 1 (ignoring the sign) = elastic: buyers react strongly, so a price rise cuts total revenue.\n\u2022 Smaller than 1 = inelastic: buyers barely react, so a price rise increases total revenue.\n\nExam tip: never stop at the number. Say what it means for the firm's revenue or for the government's tax decision. That interpretation is where the marks are."},{q:"How do I structure a 20-mark essay?",a:"For a 20-mark \"evaluate\" or \"discuss\" essay, examiners reward a judgement, not a list. Use this plan:\n\n\u2022 Introduction: define the key terms and say what the answer depends on.\n\u2022 Paragraph 1: your strongest argument for, with a labelled diagram and a full chain of reasoning.\n\u2022 Paragraph 2: a second argument, ending with \"however, this depends on\u2026\".\n\u2022 Paragraph 3: the best argument against, or an alternative policy, weighed against the first.\n\u2022 Conclusion: answer the exact question, and state the condition that decides it (the size of the elasticity, the time period, the cause of the problem).\n\nThe most common way to lose marks is leaving all the evaluation to the last paragraph. Evaluate at the end of every point."},{q:"Demand-pull vs cost-push inflation",a:"Both raise the price level, but they start on opposite sides of the AD/AS diagram.\n\n\u2022 Demand-pull: aggregate demand shifts right (lower interest rates, tax cuts, a boom in exports). Prices rise and real output rises, at least in the short run.\n\u2022 Cost-push: short-run aggregate supply shifts left (dearer oil, higher wages, a weaker exchange rate raising import costs). Prices rise but real output falls.\n\nWhy it matters: the right policy depends on the cause. Higher interest rates deal with demand-pull well, but against cost-push they cut output even further. Making that distinction is exactly the evaluation that earns top-band marks."}],n="The full Lab AI Tutor is coming soon. It will answer any question from your syllabus using your board's mark schemes. For now, pick one of the sample questions below to see how it works, and bring other questions to your next class."},854,[]);